**Definition 2.3 (field partition and ownership).** Write $Y(h)$ for the
complete raw physical record of $P$: its 64 finite eight-track tape words,
all actually constructed finite control bits, including inactive versions,
actual PC/mode/cursor, latches and tokens, and fixed port origins.
Unbounded counts, names, code used by the simulator and word registers
are ordinary tape material. In particular, a later simulator's encoded
old state is its paid bank workspace, not another live source.

Use the actual finite bit locations of the prescribed primitive mechanism
and of the once-constructed $D_0,L,D_1$ versions. First order existing
primitive-mechanism locations by their declared field/bit order; then order
new locations by original boot, $L$ boot, $D_1$ boot and any later native
initialization construction, each in its prescribed installation order.
A location reused by two descriptions is listed once, by physical identity;
retained distinct version locations are distinct. This yields a finite
ordered set $C$ of size $q\ge1$. It can be enumerated from the fixed
literal mechanism and three finite expansions, without $H,u$ or a request.
The bootstrap's mode/PC/cursor range is finite because these prescribed
slot sequences are finite; it is encoded by the actual mechanism's bits,
not by a new unbounded register. No bit is added merely to record this
enumeration. Thus $Y$ has exactly the following disjoint field partition.

| Physical field | Sole storage component |
| --- | --- |
| The eight tracks of every constructed cell, including heads and seals | Its tape cell on its actual port and append chain |
| Primitive PC, mode, finite cursor, latches, tokens and finite native version material | Its single location in $C$, absent until its actual constructor if not initially present |
| Dynamic code, serialized descriptions, word-program registers, counters, manifest, banks, descriptors and package/output data | Their existing ordinary cells, with no additional finite-register copy |
| Semantic event lists, provenance, busy interpretations and logical source values | Interpretations of the preceding material and actual history; no extra runtime store |

The producer retains source/private authority on 16–23. Original actor
archives on 24–31 remain distinct. Eight actual TOKs transfer unused
verifier lanes 32–39 to $O$: banks A/B, Base scratch, length scratch,
descriptors, manifest/staging, frame stream, service archive. Asset owners
authorize and pay actual copies and public-head saves/restores; $O$ receives
no producer token. The client is the predeclared $C=A$, with receiver 40
and the separately installed invocation token. Mailbox 48 has its actual
setup, handoff and returned availability tokens. Original public heads
0–31 are preserved by the declared paid save/restore protocol. Ownership
and availability are the native token/field relations, not unencoded
mutable vertex labels. A historical permission bit remains data.

